          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My blood sugar has been over 20 all day and I feel sick. How urgent is that, and what should I be told about diabetic emergencies?",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now. Do not wait to see whether the reading comes down.** A blood sugar over 20 with nausea is on the emergency list in the diabetic emergencies guidance (red-flag-diabetic-emergency). That guidance says nausea, vomiting, or stomach pain with very high blood sugar can signal diabetic ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state. Both are life-threatening and can develop over hours to days. If someone is with you, ask them to stay with you and to tell the call handler your blood sugar reading and how you feel. If you are drowsy or hard to wake, do not try to eat or drink anything.\n\nTreat any of the following as an emergency too, and tell the call handler if you have them: vomiting or being unable to keep fluids down, stomach pain, deep, fast or laboured breathing, breath that smells fruity or like nail varnish remover, drowsiness, confusion or difficulty staying awake, severe dehydration, a very dry mouth or not passing urine, and weakness with fast breathing, especially if you have type 1 diabetes. Other guidance in this corpus also lists severe breathing difficulty and sudden severe abdominal pain as emergencies.\n\nSome readings are given in mmol/L and others in mg/dL, so check which unit your meter uses when you describe the number. The sources I checked do not explain in detail what causes these conditions, and they do not say what to do while waiting for help. Ask the emergency operator or a clinician what to do in the meantime. If you take diabetes medicines, tell the clinician, but do not change or stop any medicine on your own.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 14665.323589000081,
